of shopping tesco's  estimates goes up by  three to five percent i mean it's not  the sunny the sunny  the sunny uplands that have been  described in the past a degree of  a degree of honesty about the economic  shock that we're going to experience  from january 1st onwards is surely  necessary as well  yes but i don't believe any of these  forecasts are correct every one of these  by the way produced by the bank of  england  or by the treasury has been  fundamentally out now for ages  and it all depends what you assume to be  the case of a uk  after it leaves the european union and  then trade separately  and all of their assumptions are that  other trade arrangements won't make any  great difference  yet if you look at trade deals done by  places like australia with the united  states they had a huge  impact on their economy made it grow  faster  so and i really do i'm skeptical because  there's a lot of scare mongering going  on the truth is  that the uk is uh the fourth largest  uh export destiny for the rest of the  world the fourth largest in the world uh  and  we export now uh dramatically huge  amounts but we also export  to many many countries our growing  exports in services by the way  uh go all over the world and the other  point about this deal which is not just  the way the european union's behaving  but they also refuse to have financial  services  in their deal which is our biggest  exporter so you can see straight away  it's a very one-way street  no country would accept that the other  country or the other group  would control the laws ruling the uh  their regulations  would be able to change their  regulations and make  the uk stay with those regulations  regardless even though we have no say in  it  and that would be adjudicated by the  european court of justice in what world  were those who voted for exit uh going  to be satisfied by that of course they  weren't  i think the thing about this by the way  which is really interesting is if people  didn't know why people voted to leave  the behavior of the european union right  now demonstrates exactly  why it's desperate to control everybody  it's desperate not to have competition  it's fearful therefore of the outside  world competing with it  and we know looking forward even under  eu figures they will only share about  four to  five percent of global growth over the  next 20 to 25 years so the markets  and the future is outside the european  union ian duncan smith thank you so much  for joining us in the program this  morning and  do have a merry christmas when it comes  and you very much
 

